{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AX1SpNSwkb3DBQhXtvhQ3W","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→---\n     2→title: The 8 Best Timezone Converter Tools in 2025\n     3→description: Looking for reliable timezone conversion? We tested dozens of tools to find the best options for remote teams, travelers, and global businesses in 2025.\n     4→date: 2025-09-14\n     5→author: Site Team\n     6→tags: [timezone tools, productivity, remote work]\n     7→keywords: best timezone converter, time zone calculator, world clock app, timezone tools 2025\n     8→image: /images/blog/timezone-tools.jpg\n     9→slug: best-timezone-tools-2025\n    10→readTime: 5 min read\n    11→featured: true\n    12→published: true\n    13→---\n    14→\n    15→Last week I scheduled a meeting for 3 PM my time, forgetting my client was in Tokyo. They joined the call at 5 AM their time – not exactly the best way to maintain a business relationship. Sound familiar?\n    16→\n    17→After testing over 30 timezone tools this month, I found the ones that actually work. Here are the 8 best options for 2025.\n    18→\n    19→## Quick Overview: Best Tools by Use Case\n    20→\n    21→- **Best Overall**: [World Time Buddy](https://www.worldtimebuddy.com/)\n    22→- **Best Free Option**: [Datetime.app](/)\n    23→- **Best for Teams**: [Every Time Zone](https://everytimezone.com/)\n    24→- **Best Mobile App**: Time Zone Converter by [TimeandDate](https://www.timeanddate.com/)\n    25→- **Best for Developers**: [Spacetime](https://github.com/spencermountain/spacetime)\n    26→\n    27→## 1. [World Time Buddy](https://www.worldtimebuddy.com/)\n    28→\n    29→[World Time Buddy](https://www.worldtimebuddy.com/) remains the gold standard for timezone conversion. The interface shows multiple time zones side by side with a draggable time slider that instantly shows corresponding times across all zones.\n    30→\n    31→**Pros:**\n    32→- Visual timeline makes scheduling intuitive\n    33→- Handles DST changes automatically\n    34→- Free version covers most needs\n    35→- Mobile apps available\n    36→\n    37→**Cons:**\n    38→- Premium features cost $39/year\n    39→- Can be slow with many timezones\n    40→- Limited customization options\n    41→\n    42→**Best for:** Freelancers and small teams scheduling regular international calls.\n    43→\n    44→**Pricing:** Free for 3 timezones, $39/year for unlimited\n    45→\n    46→## 2. [TimeandDate.com](https://www.timeanddate.com/worldclock/converter.html)\n    47→\n    48→[TimeandDate.com](https://www.timeanddate.com/) offers comprehensive timezone features beyond simple conversion. Their meeting planner specifically helps find optimal meeting times across multiple zones.\n    49→\n    50→**Pros:**\n    51→- Completely free\n    52→- Extensive database of cities\n    53→- Shows DST transitions clearly\n    54→- No registration required\n    55→\n    56→**Cons:**\n    57→- Interface feels dated\n    58→- Ads can be distracting\n    59→- Mobile experience needs work\n    60→\n    61→**Best for:** Quick one-off conversions and checking specific city times.\n    62→\n    63→**Pricing:** Free with ads\n    64→\n    65→## 3. [Datetime.app](/)\n    66→\n    67→We built [Datetime.app](/) after getting frustrated with overcomplicated tools. It focuses on instant timezone conversion with a clean, modern interface that works perfectly on all devices.\n    68→\n    69→**Pros:**\n    70→- Lightning-fast performance\n    71→- No ads or signup required\n    72→- Built-in [world clock](/) and [calendar](/calendar/2025)\n    73→- Automatic DST handling\n    74→- [Age calculator](/age-calculator) and [holiday calendar](/holidays) included\n    75→\n    76→**Cons:**\n    77→- Newer tool (launched 2024)\n    78→- Fewer advanced features than some competitors\n    79→\n    80→**Best for:** Anyone who wants simple, fast timezone conversion without the bloat.\n    81→\n    82→**Pricing:** Completely free\n    83→\n    84→## 4. [Every Time Zone](https://everytimezone.com/)\n    85→\n    86→[Every Time Zone](https://everytimezone.com/) displays all timezones on a single screen with color-coded bands showing business hours, making it perfect for distributed teams.\n    87→\n    88→**Pros:**\n    89→- See all zones at once\n    90→- Color coding for work hours\n    91→- Clean, minimal design\n    92→- No setup required\n    93→\n    94→**Cons:**\n    95→- No mobile app\n    96→- Can't save custom zone sets\n    97→- Limited to current time\n    98→\n    99→**Best for:** Remote teams needing quick visual reference for global time differences.\n   100→\n   101→**Pricing:** Free\n   102→\n   103→## 5. [Slack's](https://slack.com/) Built-in Timezone Feature\n   104→\n   105→If your team uses [Slack](https://slack.com/), you already have a solid timezone tool. Hover over any teammate's name to see their local time, or use `/timezone` commands.\n   106→\n   107→**Pros:**\n   108→- Integrated where you work\n   109→- Automatic team timezone tracking\n   110→- No extra tools needed\n   111→- Shows working hours\n   112→\n   113→**Cons:**\n   114→- Slack subscription required\n   115→- Limited to team members\n   116→- Basic conversion features\n   117→\n   118→**Best for:** Teams already using Slack for communication.\n   119→\n   120→**Pricing:** Included with Slack (from $8.75/user/month)\n   121→\n   122→## 6. [Time Zone Converter by 24TimeZones](https://24timezones.com/)\n   123→\n   124→[24TimeZones](https://24timezones.com/) converter excels at bulk conversions and includes a useful timezone map for visual reference.\n   125→\n   126→**Pros:**\n   127→- Convert multiple times at once\n   128→- Interactive world map\n   129→- Meeting planner included\n   130→- Email results feature\n   131→\n   132→**Cons:**\n   133→- Heavy advertising\n   134→- Cluttered interface\n   135→- Slow loading times\n   136→\n   137→**Best for:** Occasional users who need visual timezone maps.\n   138→\n   139→**Pricing:** Free with ads\n   140→\n   141→## 7. [Spacetime](https://github.com/spencermountain/spacetime) (for Developers)\n   142→\n   143→Developers working with timezones in code need [Spacetime](https://github.com/spencermountain/spacetime). This JavaScript library handles timezone math with a simple, intuitive API.\n   144→\n   145→**Pros:**\n   146→- Lightweight (35kb)\n   147→- Immutable and chainable\n   148→- Handles DST automatically\n   149→- Great documentation\n   150→\n   151→**Cons:**\n   152→- Requires coding knowledge\n   153→- JavaScript only\n   154→- Not for end users\n   155→\n   156→**Best for:** Developers building timezone features into applications.\n   157→\n   158→**Pricing:** Free (open source)\n   159→\n   160→## 8. [Clockwise](https://www.getclockwise.com/)\n   161→\n   162→[Clockwise](https://www.getclockwise.com/) uses AI to automatically find the best meeting times across time zones while protecting everyone's focus time. It integrates with Google Calendar and Slack to optimize team schedules.\n   163→\n   164→**Pros:**\n   165→- AI-powered scheduling optimization\n   166→- Protects focus time automatically\n   167→- Smart meeting suggestions\n   168→- Slack and Calendar integration\n   169→- Team analytics dashboard\n   170→\n   171→**Cons:**\n   172→- Requires Google Workspace\n   173→- Learning curve for advanced features\n   174→- Premium features can be expensive\n   175→\n   176→**Best for:** Teams that value deep work and want AI-assisted scheduling.\n   177→\n   178→**Pricing:** Free for individuals, $6.75/user/month for teams\n   179→\n   180→## How to Choose the Right Tool\n   181→\n   182→Consider these factors when selecting a timezone converter:\n   183→\n   184→1. **Frequency of use**: Daily users need bookmarkable tools or apps\n   185→2. **Team size**: Larger teams benefit from integrated solutions\n   186→3. **Budget**: Free tools work fine for most individual needs\n   187→4. **Platform**: Ensure mobile support if you travel frequently\n   188→5. **Privacy**: Some tools require calendar access or registration\n   189→\n   190→## Final Thoughts\n   191→\n   192→After extensive testing, most people will be perfectly served by free tools like [Datetime.app](/) or [TimeandDate.com](https://www.timeanddate.com/). Teams should consider integrated solutions like [Slack's](https://slack.com/) timezone features or [Clockwise](https://www.getclockwise.com/).\n   193→\n   194→The \"best\" tool ultimately depends on your specific workflow. I personally use [Datetime.app](/) for quick conversions and [World Time Buddy](https://www.worldtimebuddy.com/) for complex scheduling. Try a few options and see what sticks – your future meeting attendees will thank you for getting the time right.\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"84423f22-f1d0-4544-bb63-fefbb18abb91","uuid":"24a47a5a-82d1-4bec-951f-21885267368f","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/airyland/workspace/becool/datetime/content/blog/best-timezone-tools-2025.md","content":"---\ntitle: The 8 Best Timezone Converter Tools in 2025\ndescription: Looking for reliable timezone conversion? We tested dozens of tools to find the best options for remote teams, travelers, and global businesses in 2025.\ndate: 2025-09-14\nauthor: Site Team\ntags: [timezone tools, productivity, remote work]\nkeywords: best timezone converter, time zone calculator, world clock app, timezone tools 2025\nimage: /images/blog/timezone-tools.jpg\nslug: best-timezone-tools-2025\nreadTime: 5 min read\nfeatured: true\npublished: true\n---\n\nLast week I scheduled a meeting for 3 PM my time, forgetting my client was in Tokyo. They joined the call at 5 AM their time – not exactly the best way to maintain a business relationship. Sound familiar?\n\nAfter testing over 30 timezone tools this month, I found the ones that actually work. Here are the 8 best options for 2025.\n\n## Quick Overview: Best Tools by Use Case\n\n- **Best Overall**: [World Time Buddy](https://www.worldtimebuddy.com/)\n- **Best Free Option**: [Datetime.app](/)\n- **Best for Teams**: [Every Time Zone](https://everytimezone.com/)\n- **Best Mobile App**: Time Zone Converter by [TimeandDate](https://www.timeanddate.com/)\n- **Best for Developers**: [Spacetime](https://github.com/spencermountain/spacetime)\n\n## 1. [World Time Buddy](https://www.worldtimebuddy.com/)\n\n[World Time Buddy](https://www.worldtimebuddy.com/) remains the gold standard for timezone conversion. The interface shows multiple time zones side by side with a draggable time slider that instantly shows corresponding times across all zones.\n\n**Pros:**\n- Visual timeline makes scheduling intuitive\n- Handles DST changes automatically\n- Free version covers most needs\n- Mobile apps available\n\n**Cons:**\n- Premium features cost $39/year\n- Can be slow with many timezones\n- Limited customization options\n\n**Best for:** Freelancers and small teams scheduling regular international calls.\n\n**Pricing:** Free for 3 timezones, $39/year for unlimited\n\n## 2. [TimeandDate.com](https://www.timeanddate.com/worldclock/converter.html)\n\n[TimeandDate.com](https://www.timeanddate.com/) offers comprehensive timezone features beyond simple conversion. Their meeting planner specifically helps find optimal meeting times across multiple zones.\n\n**Pros:**\n- Completely free\n- Extensive database of cities\n- Shows DST transitions clearly\n- No registration required\n\n**Cons:**\n- Interface feels dated\n- Ads can be distracting\n- Mobile experience needs work\n\n**Best for:** Quick one-off conversions and checking specific city times.\n\n**Pricing:** Free with ads\n\n## 3. [Datetime.app](/)\n\nWe built [Datetime.app](/) after getting frustrated with overcomplicated tools. It focuses on instant timezone conversion with a clean, modern interface that works perfectly on all devices.\n\n**Pros:**\n- Lightning-fast performance\n- No ads or signup required\n- Built-in [world clock](/) and [calendar](/calendar/2025)\n- Automatic DST handling\n- [Age calculator](/age-calculator) and [holiday calendar](/holidays) included\n\n**Cons:**\n- Newer tool (launched 2024)\n- Fewer advanced features than some competitors\n\n**Best for:** Anyone who wants simple, fast timezone conversion without the bloat.\n\n**Pricing:** Completely free\n\n## 4. [Every Time Zone](https://everytimezone.com/)\n\n[Every Time Zone](https://everytimezone.com/) displays all timezones on a single screen with color-coded bands showing business hours, making it perfect for distributed teams.\n\n**Pros:**\n- See all zones at once\n- Color coding for work hours\n- Clean, minimal design\n- No setup required\n\n**Cons:**\n- No mobile app\n- Can't save custom zone sets\n- Limited to current time\n\n**Best for:** Remote teams needing quick visual reference for global time differences.\n\n**Pricing:** Free\n\n## 5. [Slack's](https://slack.com/) Built-in Timezone Feature\n\nIf your team uses [Slack](https://slack.com/), you already have a solid timezone tool. Hover over any teammate's name to see their local time, or use `/timezone` commands.\n\n**Pros:**\n- Integrated where you work\n- Automatic team timezone tracking\n- No extra tools needed\n- Shows working hours\n\n**Cons:**\n- Slack subscription required\n- Limited to team members\n- Basic conversion features\n\n**Best for:** Teams already using Slack for communication.\n\n**Pricing:** Included with Slack (from $8.75/user/month)\n\n## 6. [Time Zone Converter by 24TimeZones](https://24timezones.com/)\n\n[24TimeZones](https://24timezones.com/) converter excels at bulk conversions and includes a useful timezone map for visual reference.\n\n**Pros:**\n- Convert multiple times at once\n- Interactive world map\n- Meeting planner included\n- Email results feature\n\n**Cons:**\n- Heavy advertising\n- Cluttered interface\n- Slow loading times\n\n**Best for:** Occasional users who need visual timezone maps.\n\n**Pricing:** Free with ads\n\n## 7. [Spacetime](https://github.com/spencermountain/spacetime) (for Developers)\n\nDevelopers working with timezones in code need [Spacetime](https://github.com/spencermountain/spacetime). This JavaScript library handles timezone math with a simple, intuitive API.\n\n**Pros:**\n- Lightweight (35kb)\n- Immutable and chainable\n- Handles DST automatically\n- Great documentation\n\n**Cons:**\n- Requires coding knowledge\n- JavaScript only\n- Not for end users\n\n**Best for:** Developers building timezone features into applications.\n\n**Pricing:** Free (open source)\n\n## 8. [Clockwise](https://www.getclockwise.com/)\n\n[Clockwise](https://www.getclockwise.com/) uses AI to automatically find the best meeting times across time zones while protecting everyone's focus time. It integrates with Google Calendar and Slack to optimize team schedules.\n\n**Pros:**\n- AI-powered scheduling optimization\n- Protects focus time automatically\n- Smart meeting suggestions\n- Slack and Calendar integration\n- Team analytics dashboard\n\n**Cons:**\n- Requires Google Workspace\n- Learning curve for advanced features\n- Premium features can be expensive\n\n**Best for:** Teams that value deep work and want AI-assisted scheduling.\n\n**Pricing:** Free for individuals, $6.75/user/month for teams\n\n## How to Choose the Right Tool\n\nConsider these factors when selecting a timezone converter:\n\n1. **Frequency of use**: Daily users need bookmarkable tools or apps\n2. **Team size**: Larger teams benefit from integrated solutions\n3. **Budget**: Free tools work fine for most individual needs\n4. **Platform**: Ensure mobile support if you travel frequently\n5. **Privacy**: Some tools require calendar access or registration\n\n## Final Thoughts\n\nAfter extensive testing, most people will be perfectly served by free tools like [Datetime.app](/) or [TimeandDate.com](https://www.timeanddate.com/). Teams should consider integrated solutions like [Slack's](https://slack.com/) timezone features or [Clockwise](https://www.getclockwise.com/).\n\nThe \"best\" tool ultimately depends on your specific workflow. I personally use [Datetime.app](/) for quick conversions and [World Time Buddy](https://www.worldtimebuddy.com/) for complex scheduling. Try a few options and see what sticks – your future meeting attendees will thank you for getting the time right.","numLines":194,"startLine":1,"totalLines":194}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016cBM9ACudvcDhuyETQJzAZ","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→---\n     2→title: Understanding Time Zones Once and For All: GMT, UTC, CST, and Daylight Saving Time\n     3→description: Confused by GMT, UTC, and CST? You're not alone. Here's a plain-English guide to understanding time zones, why they exist, and how to stop getting tripped up by them.\n     4→date: 2025-12-10\n     5→author: Site Team\n     6→tags: [time zones, UTC, GMT, DST, guide]\n     7→keywords: UTC time, GMT vs UTC difference, CST time zone, daylight saving time explained, what is UTC, time zone abbreviations\n     8→image: /images/blog/timezone-guide.jpg\n     9→slug: understanding-time-zones-gmt-utc-cst-dst\n    10→readTime: 6 min read\n    11→featured: true\n    12→published: true\n    13→---\n    14→\n    15→So you scheduled a meeting at 3 PM CST. Great. Except your colleague in Beijing thinks CST means China Standard Time, and your friend in Chicago thinks it's Central Standard Time. Now you've got two people joining a call 14 hours apart.\n    16→\n    17→Welcome to the wonderful world of timezone chaos.\n    18→\n    19→If you've ever Googled \"what is UTC\" or wondered why everyone keeps using different three-letter abbreviations for time, this guide is for you. Let's untangle this mess.\n    20→\n    21→## The Two Big Standards: GMT and UTC\n    22→\n    23→You'll see GMT and UTC used interchangeably all the time. They show the same time on the clock. But they're actually different things.\n    24→\n    25→**GMT (Greenwich Mean Time)** is the old-school approach. It's based on the position of the sun at the Royal Observatory in Greenwich, England. Scientists started using it back in the 1850s to coordinate train schedules across Britain. The problem? Earth's rotation isn't perfectly consistent—it actually wobbles and slows down over time.\n    26→\n    27→**UTC (Coordinated Universal Time)** is the modern replacement. Instead of relying on the sun, UTC uses about 400 atomic clocks around the world that measure time by counting vibrations of cesium atoms. These clocks are ridiculously precise—they'd lose less than one second over 300 million years.\n    28→\n    29→Here's the practical difference: because Earth's rotation is slightly unpredictable, UTC occasionally adds a \"leap second\" to stay in sync with solar time. The last one was added in December 2016.\n    30→\n    31→**When should you use which?**\n    32→- Use GMT when referring to local time in the UK during winter\n    33→- Use UTC for anything technical—aviation, computing, international coordination\n    34→- In casual conversation, they're effectively the same\n    35→\n    36→## The CST Problem (And Other Ambiguous Abbreviations)\n    37→\n    38→Here's where things get messy. CST doesn't mean one thing—it means at least three:\n    39→\n    40→| Abbreviation | Full Name | UTC Offset |\n    41→|--------------|-----------|------------|\n    42→| CST | Central Standard Time (US) | UTC-6 |\n    43→| CST | China Standard Time | UTC+8 |\n    44→| CST | Cuba Standard Time | UTC-5 |\n    45→\n    46→That's a 14-hour gap between American CST and Chinese CST. Schedule a meeting at \"2 PM CST\" without specifying which one, and you're rolling the dice.\n    47→\n    48→And it gets worse. EST can mean:\n    49→- Eastern Standard Time (US and Canada) at UTC-5\n    50→- Eastern Standard Time (Australia) at UTC+10\n    51→- Eastern Summer Time (Australia) at UTC+11\n    52→\n    53→This isn't a theoretical problem. Developers have shipped bugs because of this exact confusion. One [notable case](https://bugs.launchpad.net/bugs/82230) on Launchpad involved Australian users being confused when times were cited as EST during summer, with no way to know which offset was intended.\n    54→\n    55→**The solution?** Stop using abbreviations when precision matters. Instead:\n    56→- Say \"2 PM Eastern US time\"\n    57→- Use UTC offsets: \"14:00 UTC-5\"\n    58→- Use IANA timezone identifiers: \"America/New_York\" or \"Asia/Shanghai\"\n    59→\n    60→## Daylight Saving Time: Why Clocks Jump Around\n    61→\n    62→Twice a year, about 70 countries play the \"move your clock\" game. In the US, clocks spring forward one hour on the second Sunday in March and fall back on the first Sunday in November.\n    63→\n    64→The original idea was to save energy—if people wake up and go to bed with the sun, they'll use less artificial lighting. This made sense during World War I when it was first widely adopted. Today, the actual [energy savings are debatable](https://www.sleepfoundation.org/circadian-rhythm/daylight-saving-time).\n    65→\n    66→**The weird parts:**\n    67→\n    68→1. **Not everyone does it.** Hawaii and most of Arizona stay on standard time year-round. Same with Japan, China, and most of the equatorial world.\n    69→\n    70→2. **It creates ghost times and duplicate times.** When clocks spring forward from 2:00 AM to 3:00 AM, there's no 2:30 AM that day. When they fall back, 1:30 AM happens twice in the same morning.\n    71→\n    72→3. **Different countries change on different dates.** The US and UK don't sync up—the UK changes clocks on the last Sunday of March and October. This means the time difference between New York and London is 5 hours for most of the year, but temporarily becomes 4 or 6 hours during the transition weeks.\n    73→\n    74→4. **The Southern Hemisphere is opposite.** When it's summer in Australia, it's winter in North America. So \"summer time\" means different months depending on where you are.\n    75→\n    76→## What Developers (and Everyone Else) Should Know\n    77→\n    78→If you work with time across borders—whether you're a developer, a remote worker, or just someone scheduling calls with overseas friends—here are the rules that will save you headaches:\n    79→\n    80→**Store everything in UTC.** Your database should save timestamps in UTC. Your logs should be in UTC. Convert to local time only when displaying to users.\n    81→\n    82→**Use proper timezone identifiers.** \"America/New_York\" is unambiguous. \"EST\" is not. The [IANA timezone database](https://www.iana.org/time-zones) maintains the canonical list, and it's what your phone, computer, and web browser use under the hood.\n    83→\n    84→**Never hardcode UTC offsets.** New York is UTC-5, right? Only in winter. In summer it's UTC-4. Russia has changed its UTC offset multiple times in the past decade. Political decisions change timezones more often than you'd expect.\n    85→\n    86→**Test around DST transitions.** If your app handles scheduling, test what happens when someone books a meeting for 2:30 AM on the day clocks spring forward. Does it crash? Show an error? Silently pick a different time?\n    87→\n    88→## Quick Reference: Common Offsets\n    89→\n    90→Here's a cheat sheet for the time zones you'll encounter most often:\n    91→\n    92→| Location | Standard Time | Daylight Time |\n    93→|----------|---------------|---------------|\n    94→| US Pacific (LA) | UTC-8 (PST) | UTC-7 (PDT) |\n    95→| US Eastern (NY) | UTC-5 (EST) | UTC-4 (EDT) |\n    96→| UK (London) | UTC+0 (GMT) | UTC+1 (BST) |\n    97→| Central Europe | UTC+1 (CET) | UTC+2 (CEST) |\n    98→| Japan (Tokyo) | UTC+9 (JST) | No DST |\n    99→| China (Beijing) | UTC+8 (CST) | No DST |\n   100→| Australia Eastern | UTC+10 (AEST) | UTC+11 (AEDT) |\n   101→\n   102→## Wrapping Up\n   103→\n   104→Time zones exist because the sun rises at different times in different places—there's no getting around that. The complexity comes from abbreviations that mean multiple things, political decisions about daylight saving, and software that assumes time is simpler than it actually is.\n   105→\n   106→The short version:\n   107→- GMT and UTC are basically the same for everyday use\n   108→- Never trust three-letter abbreviations without context\n   109→- Daylight Saving Time changes when, not everywhere, and not on the same dates\n   110→- When in doubt, use UTC with a clear offset\n   111→\n   112→Need to convert times right now? Our [timezone converter](/) handles all of this automatically. Or check the [current UTC time](/utc) if you just need a reference point.\n   113→\n   114→## References\n   115→\n   116→1. [UTC & GMT – Same Difference?](https://www.timeanddate.com/time/gmt-utc-time.html) - TimeandDate.com\n   117→2. [Coordinated Universal Time](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coordinated_Universal_Time) - Wikipedia\n   118→3. [IANA Time Zone Database](https://www.iana.org/time-zones) - IANA\n   119→4. [Falsehoods Programmers Believe About Time Zones](https://www.zainrizvi.io/blog/falsehoods-programmers-believe-about-time-zones/) - Zain Rizvi\n   120→5. [Daylight Saving Time Explained](https://www.sleepfoundation.org/circadian-rhythm/daylight-saving-time) - Sleep Foundation\n   121→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"84423f22-f1d0-4544-bb63-fefbb18abb91","uuid":"9aed26a1-57e0-4ce2-9f97-e2bfb3974b9f","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/airyland/workspace/becool/datetime/content/blog/understanding-time-zones-gmt-utc-cst-dst.md","content":"---\ntitle: Understanding Time Zones Once and For All: GMT, UTC, CST, and Daylight Saving Time\ndescription: Confused by GMT, UTC, and CST? You're not alone. Here's a plain-English guide to understanding time zones, why they exist, and how to stop getting tripped up by them.\ndate: 2025-12-10\nauthor: Site Team\ntags: [time zones, UTC, GMT, DST, guide]\nkeywords: UTC time, GMT vs UTC difference, CST time zone, daylight saving time explained, what is UTC, time zone abbreviations\nimage: /images/blog/timezone-guide.jpg\nslug: understanding-time-zones-gmt-utc-cst-dst\nreadTime: 6 min read\nfeatured: true\npublished: true\n---\n\nSo you scheduled a meeting at 3 PM CST. Great. Except your colleague in Beijing thinks CST means China Standard Time, and your friend in Chicago thinks it's Central Standard Time. Now you've got two people joining a call 14 hours apart.\n\nWelcome to the wonderful world of timezone chaos.\n\nIf you've ever Googled \"what is UTC\" or wondered why everyone keeps using different three-letter abbreviations for time, this guide is for you. Let's untangle this mess.\n\n## The Two Big Standards: GMT and UTC\n\nYou'll see GMT and UTC used interchangeably all the time. They show the same time on the clock. But they're actually different things.\n\n**GMT (Greenwich Mean Time)** is the old-school approach. It's based on the position of the sun at the Royal Observatory in Greenwich, England. Scientists started using it back in the 1850s to coordinate train schedules across Britain. The problem? Earth's rotation isn't perfectly consistent—it actually wobbles and slows down over time.\n\n**UTC (Coordinated Universal Time)** is the modern replacement. Instead of relying on the sun, UTC uses about 400 atomic clocks around the world that measure time by counting vibrations of cesium atoms. These clocks are ridiculously precise—they'd lose less than one second over 300 million years.\n\nHere's the practical difference: because Earth's rotation is slightly unpredictable, UTC occasionally adds a \"leap second\" to stay in sync with solar time. The last one was added in December 2016.\n\n**When should you use which?**\n- Use GMT when referring to local time in the UK during winter\n- Use UTC for anything technical—aviation, computing, international coordination\n- In casual conversation, they're effectively the same\n\n## The CST Problem (And Other Ambiguous Abbreviations)\n\nHere's where things get messy. CST doesn't mean one thing—it means at least three:\n\n| Abbreviation | Full Name | UTC Offset |\n|--------------|-----------|------------|\n| CST | Central Standard Time (US) | UTC-6 |\n| CST | China Standard Time | UTC+8 |\n| CST | Cuba Standard Time | UTC-5 |\n\nThat's a 14-hour gap between American CST and Chinese CST. Schedule a meeting at \"2 PM CST\" without specifying which one, and you're rolling the dice.\n\nAnd it gets worse. EST can mean:\n- Eastern Standard Time (US and Canada) at UTC-5\n- Eastern Standard Time (Australia) at UTC+10\n- Eastern Summer Time (Australia) at UTC+11\n\nThis isn't a theoretical problem. Developers have shipped bugs because of this exact confusion. One [notable case](https://bugs.launchpad.net/bugs/82230) on Launchpad involved Australian users being confused when times were cited as EST during summer, with no way to know which offset was intended.\n\n**The solution?** Stop using abbreviations when precision matters. Instead:\n- Say \"2 PM Eastern US time\"\n- Use UTC offsets: \"14:00 UTC-5\"\n- Use IANA timezone identifiers: \"America/New_York\" or \"Asia/Shanghai\"\n\n## Daylight Saving Time: Why Clocks Jump Around\n\nTwice a year, about 70 countries play the \"move your clock\" game. In the US, clocks spring forward one hour on the second Sunday in March and fall back on the first Sunday in November.\n\nThe original idea was to save energy—if people wake up and go to bed with the sun, they'll use less artificial lighting. This made sense during World War I when it was first widely adopted. Today, the actual [energy savings are debatable](https://www.sleepfoundation.org/circadian-rhythm/daylight-saving-time).\n\n**The weird parts:**\n\n1. **Not everyone does it.** Hawaii and most of Arizona stay on standard time year-round. Same with Japan, China, and most of the equatorial world.\n\n2. **It creates ghost times and duplicate times.** When clocks spring forward from 2:00 AM to 3:00 AM, there's no 2:30 AM that day. When they fall back, 1:30 AM happens twice in the same morning.\n\n3. **Different countries change on different dates.** The US and UK don't sync up—the UK changes clocks on the last Sunday of March and October. This means the time difference between New York and London is 5 hours for most of the year, but temporarily becomes 4 or 6 hours during the transition weeks.\n\n4. **The Southern Hemisphere is opposite.** When it's summer in Australia, it's winter in North America. So \"summer time\" means different months depending on where you are.\n\n## What Developers (and Everyone Else) Should Know\n\nIf you work with time across borders—whether you're a developer, a remote worker, or just someone scheduling calls with overseas friends—here are the rules that will save you headaches:\n\n**Store everything in UTC.** Your database should save timestamps in UTC. Your logs should be in UTC. Convert to local time only when displaying to users.\n\n**Use proper timezone identifiers.** \"America/New_York\" is unambiguous. \"EST\" is not. The [IANA timezone database](https://www.iana.org/time-zones) maintains the canonical list, and it's what your phone, computer, and web browser use under the hood.\n\n**Never hardcode UTC offsets.** New York is UTC-5, right? Only in winter. In summer it's UTC-4. Russia has changed its UTC offset multiple times in the past decade. Political decisions change timezones more often than you'd expect.\n\n**Test around DST transitions.** If your app handles scheduling, test what happens when someone books a meeting for 2:30 AM on the day clocks spring forward. Does it crash? Show an error? Silently pick a different time?\n\n## Quick Reference: Common Offsets\n\nHere's a cheat sheet for the time zones you'll encounter most often:\n\n| Location | Standard Time | Daylight Time |\n|----------|---------------|---------------|\n| US Pacific (LA) | UTC-8 (PST) | UTC-7 (PDT) |\n| US Eastern (NY) | UTC-5 (EST) | UTC-4 (EDT) |\n| UK (London) | UTC+0 (GMT) | UTC+1 (BST) |\n| Central Europe | UTC+1 (CET) | UTC+2 (CEST) |\n| Japan (Tokyo) | UTC+9 (JST) | No DST |\n| China (Beijing) | UTC+8 (CST) | No DST |\n| Australia Eastern | UTC+10 (AEST) | UTC+11 (AEDT) |\n\n## Wrapping Up\n\nTime zones exist because the sun rises at different times in different places—there's no getting around that. The complexity comes from abbreviations that mean multiple things, political decisions about daylight saving, and software that assumes time is simpler than it actually is.\n\nThe short version:\n- GMT and UTC are basically the same for everyday use\n- Never trust three-letter abbreviations without context\n- Daylight Saving Time changes when, not everywhere, and not on the same dates\n- When in doubt, use UTC with a clear offset\n\nNeed to convert times right now? Our [timezone converter](/) handles all of this automatically. Or check the [current UTC time](/utc) if you just need a reference point.\n\n## References\n\n1. [UTC & GMT – Same Difference?](https://www.timeanddate.com/time/gmt-utc-time.html) - TimeandDate.com\n2. [Coordinated Universal Time](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coordinated_Universal_Time) - Wikipedia\n3. [IANA Time Zone Database](https://www.iana.org/time-zones) - IANA\n4. [Falsehoods Programmers Believe About Time Zones](https://www.zainrizvi.io/blog/falsehoods-programmers-believe-about-time-zones/) - Zain Rizvi\n5. [Daylight Saving Time Explained](https://www.sleepfoundation.org/circadian-rhythm/daylight-saving-time) - Sleep Foundation\n","numLines":121,"startLine":1,"totalLines":121}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CqqqYLokkY46as26TTAm2E","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→const fs = require('fs');\n     2→const path = require('path');\n     3→\n     4→const contentDir = path.join(process.cwd(), 'content', 'blog');\n     5→const outputDir = path.join(process.cwd(), 'data', 'blog');\n     6→\n     7→// Ensure output directory exists\n     8→if (!fs.existsSync(outputDir)) {\n     9→  fs.mkdirSync(outputDir, { recursive: true });\n    10→}\n    11→\n    12→// Simple frontmatter parser\n    13→function parseFrontmatter(content) {\n    14→  const lines = content.split('\\n');\n    15→  const frontmatter = {};\n    16→  let inFrontmatter = false;\n    17→  let contentStartIndex = 0;\n    18→\n    19→  for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {\n    20→    const line = lines[i];\n    21→\n    22→    if (i === 0 && line === '---') {\n    23→      inFrontmatter = true;\n    24→      continue;\n    25→    }\n    26→\n    27→    if (inFrontmatter && line === '---') {\n    28→      contentStartIndex = i + 1;\n    29→      break;\n    30→    }\n    31→\n    32→    if (inFrontmatter) {\n    33→      const match = line.match(/^(\\w+):\\s*(.*)$/);\n    34→      if (match) {\n    35→        const key = match[1];\n    36→        let value = match[2].trim();\n    37→\n    38→        // Handle arrays (tags)\n    39→        if (value.startsWith('[') && value.endsWith(']')) {\n    40→          value = value.slice(1, -1).split(',').map(v => v.trim().replace(/[\"']/g, ''));\n    41→        } else {\n    42→          // Remove quotes if present\n    43→          value = value.replace(/^[\"']|[\"']$/g, '');\n    44→        }\n    45→\n    46→        frontmatter[key] = value;\n    47→      }\n    48→    }\n    49→  }\n    50→\n    51→  const markdownContent = lines.slice(contentStartIndex).join('\\n');\n    52→  return { frontmatter, content: markdownContent };\n    53→}\n    54→\n    55→// Improved markdown to HTML converter\n    56→function markdownToHtml(markdown) {\n    57→  let html = markdown;\n    58→\n    59→  // Preserve code blocks first\n    60→  const codeBlocks = [];\n    61→  html = html.replace(/```([\\s\\S]*?)```/g, (match, code) => {\n    62→    const placeholder = `__CODE_BLOCK_${codeBlocks.length}__`;\n    63→    codeBlocks.push('<pre><code>' + escapeHtml(code.trim()) + '</code></pre>');\n    64→    return placeholder;\n    65→  });\n    66→\n    67→  // Inline code\n    68→  html = html.replace(/`([^`]+)`/g, '<code>$1</code>');\n    69→\n    70→  // Headers (shift levels: # becomes h2, ## becomes h3, etc.)\n    71→  html = html.replace(/^###### (.*?)$/gm, '<h6>$1</h6>');\n    72→  html = html.replace(/^##### (.*?)$/gm, '<h6>$1</h6>');\n    73→  html = html.replace(/^#### (.*?)$/gm, '<h5>$1</h5>');\n    74→  html = html.replace(/^### (.*?)$/gm, '<h4>$1</h4>');\n    75→  html = html.replace(/^## (.*?)$/gm, '<h3>$1</h3>');\n    76→  html = html.replace(/^# (.*?)$/gm, '<h2>$1</h2>');\n    77→\n    78→  // Blockquotes\n    79→  html = html.replace(/^> (.*?)$/gm, '<blockquote>$1</blockquote>');\n    80→\n    81→  // Bold and italic\n    82→  html = html.replace(/\\*\\*\\*(.*?)\\*\\*\\*/g, '<strong><em>$1</em></strong>');\n    83→  html = html.replace(/\\*\\*(.*?)\\*\\*/g, '<strong>$1</strong>');\n    84→  html = html.replace(/\\*(.*?)\\*/g, '<em>$1</em>');\n    85→\n    86→  // Links (add nofollow to external links)\n    87→  html = html.replace(/\\[([^\\]]+)\\]\\(([^)]+)\\)/g, (match, text, url) => {\n    88→    // Check if it's an external link (not starting with / or #)\n    89→    const isExternal = !url.startsWith('/') && !url.startsWith('#') && !url.includes('datetime.app');\n    90→    const rel = isExternal ? ' rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\"' : '';\n    91→    return `<a href=\"${url}\"${rel}>${text}</a>`;\n    92→  });\n    93→\n    94→  // Horizontal rules (but not table separators)\n    95→  html = html.replace(/^---$/gm, '<hr>');\n    96→  html = html.replace(/^\\*\\*\\*$/gm, '<hr>');\n    97→\n    98→  // Process tables and lists\n    99→  const lines = html.split('\\n');\n   100→  let result = [];\n   101→  let inList = false;\n   102→  let listType = '';\n   103→  let inTable = false;\n   104→  let tableRows = [];\n   105→\n   106→  for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {\n   107→    let line = lines[i];\n   108→\n   109→    // Table detection\n   110→    if (line.match(/^\\|(.+)\\|$/)) {\n   111→      // Check if it's a separator line (contains only |, -, :, and spaces)\n   112→      // A separator line has cells that only contain -, :, and spaces\n   113→      const cells = line.split('|').slice(1, -1);\n   114→      const isSeparator = cells.every(cell => /^[\\s\\-:]+$/.test(cell));\n   115→\n   116→      if (!inTable && !isSeparator) {\n   117→        // Start of table\n   118→        inTable = true;\n   119→        tableRows = [];\n   120→      }\n   121→\n   122→      if (inTable) {\n   123→        if (!isSeparator) {\n   124→          // Parse table row\n   125→          const parsedCells = cells.map(cell => cell.trim());\n   126→          tableRows.push(parsedCells);\n   127→        }\n   128→\n   129→        // Check if next line is not a table row (end of table)\n   130→        if (i + 1 >= lines.length || !lines[i + 1].match(/^\\|(.+)\\|$/)) {\n   131→          // Build HTML table\n   132→          let tableHtml = '<table>\\n';\n   133→\n   134→          if (tableRows.length > 0) {\n   135→            // First row is header\n   136→            tableHtml += '<thead>\\n<tr>\\n';\n   137→            tableRows[0].forEach(cell => {\n   138→              tableHtml += `<th>${cell}</th>\\n`;\n   139→            });\n   140→            tableHtml += '</tr>\\n</thead>\\n';\n   141→\n   142→            // Remaining rows are body\n   143→            if (tableRows.length > 1) {\n   144→              tableHtml += '<tbody>\\n';\n   145→              for (let j = 1; j < tableRows.length; j++) {\n   146→                tableHtml += '<tr>\\n';\n   147→                tableRows[j].forEach(cell => {\n   148→                  tableHtml += `<td>${cell}</td>\\n`;\n   149→                });\n   150→                tableHtml += '</tr>\\n';\n   151→              }\n   152→              tableHtml += '</tbody>\\n';\n   153→            }\n   154→          }\n   155→\n   156→          tableHtml += '</table>';\n   157→          result.push(tableHtml);\n   158→\n   159→          inTable = false;\n   160→          tableRows = [];\n   161→        }\n   162→        continue;\n   163→      }\n   164→    }\n   165→\n   166→    // Unordered list\n   167→    if (line.match(/^[\\*\\-] (.+)$/)) {\n   168→      const content = line.replace(/^[\\*\\-] (.+)$/, '$1');\n   169→      if (!inList || listType !== 'ul') {\n   170→        if (inList) result.push('</' + listType + '>');\n   171→        result.push('<ul>');\n   172→        inList = true;\n   173→        listType = 'ul';\n   174→      }\n   175→      result.push('<li>' + content + '</li>');\n   176→    }\n   177→    // Ordered list\n   178→    else if (line.match(/^\\d+\\. (.+)$/)) {\n   179→      const content = line.replace(/^\\d+\\. (.+)$/, '$1');\n   180→      if (!inList || listType !== 'ol') {\n   181→        if (inList) result.push('</' + listType + '>');\n   182→        result.push('<ol>');\n   183→        inList = true;\n   184→        listType = 'ol';\n   185→      }\n   186→      result.push('<li>' + content + '</li>');\n   187→    }\n   188→    // End of list\n   189→    else {\n   190→      if (inList && line.trim() === '') {\n   191→        result.push('</' + listType + '>');\n   192→        inList = false;\n   193→        listType = '';\n   194→      }\n   195→      result.push(line);\n   196→    }\n   197→  }\n   198→\n   199→  if (inList) {\n   200→    result.push('</' + listType + '>');\n   201→  }\n   202→\n   203→  html = result.join('\\n');\n   204→\n   205→  // Paragraphs\n   206→  const blocks = html.split(/\\n\\n+/);\n   207→  html = blocks.map(block => {\n   208→    block = block.trim();\n   209→    if (block && !block.match(/^<(h[1-6]|ul|ol|li|blockquote|pre|hr|div|p|table|__CODE_BLOCK_)/)) {\n   210→      return '<p>' + block.replace(/\\n/g, ' ') + '</p>';\n   211→    }\n   212→    return block;\n   213→  }).filter(b => b).join('\\n\\n');\n   214→\n   215→  // Restore code blocks\n   216→  codeBlocks.forEach((code, i) => {\n   217→    html = html.replace(`__CODE_BLOCK_${i}__`, code);\n   218→  });\n   219→\n   220→  return html;\n   221→}\n   222→\n   223→function escapeHtml(text) {\n   224→  const map = {\n   225→    '&': '&amp;',\n   226→    '<': '&lt;',\n   227→    '>': '&gt;',\n   228→    '\"': '&quot;',\n   229→    \"'\": '&#039;'\n   230→  };\n   231→  return text.replace(/[&<>\"']/g, m => map[m]);\n   232→}\n   233→\n   234→function generateSlug(filename) {\n   235→  return filename.replace(/\\.md$/, '').toLowerCase();\n   236→}\n   237→\n   238→function generateExcerpt(content, maxLength = 150) {\n   239→  const plainText = content.replace(/<[^>]*>/g, '').replace(/\\n+/g, ' ').trim();\n   240→  if (plainText.length <= maxLength) return plainText;\n   241→  return plainText.substring(0, maxLength).trim() + '...';\n   242→}\n   243→\n   244→function estimateReadTime(content) {\n   245→  const wordsPerMinute = 200;\n   246→  const words = content.split(/\\s+/).length;\n   247→  const minutes = Math.ceil(words / wordsPerMinute);\n   248→  return `${minutes} min read`;\n   249→}\n   250→\n   251→async function buildBlog() {\n   252→  console.log('Building blog data...');\n   253→\n   254→  // Check if content directory exists\n   255→  if (!fs.existsSync(contentDir)) {\n   256→    console.log('No blog content found. Creating directory...');\n   257→    fs.mkdirSync(contentDir, { recursive: true });\n   258→    return;\n   259→  }\n   260→\n   261→  const files = fs.readdirSync(contentDir).filter(file => file.endsWith('.md'));\n   262→\n   263→  const posts = [];\n   264→\n   265→  for (const file of files) {\n   266→    const filePath = path.join(contentDir, file);\n   267→    const fileContent = fs.readFileSync(filePath, 'utf-8');\n   268→    const { frontmatter, content } = parseFrontmatter(fileContent);\n   269→\n   270→    const html = markdownToHtml(content);\n   271→    const slug = frontmatter.slug || generateSlug(file);\n   272→    const excerpt = frontmatter.description || generateExcerpt(content);\n   273→    const readTime = frontmatter.readTime || estimateReadTime(content);\n   274→\n   275→    posts.push({\n   276→      slug,\n   277→      title: frontmatter.title,\n   278→      description: excerpt,\n   279→      date: frontmatter.date,\n   280→      author: frontmatter.author || 'Site Team',\n   281→      tags: Array.isArray(frontmatter.tags) ? frontmatter.tags : (frontmatter.tags ? [frontmatter.tags] : []),\n   282→      keywords: frontmatter.keywords || '',\n   283→      image: frontmatter.image || '/images/blog/default.jpg',\n   284→      readTime,\n   285→      featured: frontmatter.featured === 'true' || frontmatter.featured === true,\n   286→      published: frontmatter.published !== 'false' && frontmatter.published !== false,\n   287→      content: html\n   288→    });\n   289→  }\n   290→\n   291→  // Sort posts by date (newest first)\n   292→  posts.sort((a, b) => new Date(b.date) - new Date(a.date));\n   293→\n   294→  // Write posts data\n   295→  fs.writeFileSync(\n   296→    path.join(outputDir, 'posts.json'),\n   297→    JSON.stringify(posts, null, 2)\n   298→  );\n   299→\n   300→  console.log(`Built ${posts.length} blog posts`);\n   301→\n   302→  // Generate RSS feed\n   303→  generateRSSFeed(posts);\n   304→\n   305→  // Generate sitemap entries\n   306→  generateSitemapEntries(posts);\n   307→}\n   308→\n   309→function generateRSSFeed(posts) {\n   310→  const rss = `<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n   311→<rss version=\"2.0\" xmlns:atom=\"http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom\">\n   312→  <channel>\n   313→    <title>Datetime.app Blog</title>\n   314→    <description>Expert insights on timezones, time management, and productivity</description>\n   315→    <link>https://datetime.app/blog</link>\n   316→    <atom:link href=\"https://datetime.app/blog/rss.xml\" rel=\"self\" type=\"application/rss+xml\"/>\n   317→    ${posts.filter(p => p.published).slice(0, 20).map(post => `\n   318→    <item>\n   319→      <title>${escapeXml(post.title)}</title>\n   320→      <description>${escapeXml(post.description)}</description>\n   321→      <link>https://datetime.app/blog/${post.slug}</link>\n   322→      <guid>https://datetime.app/blog/${post.slug}</guid>\n   323→      <pubDate>${new Date(post.date).toUTCString()}</pubDate>\n   324→    </item>`).join('')}\n   325→  </channel>\n   326→</rss>`;\n   327→\n   328→  fs.writeFileSync(path.join(outputDir, 'rss.xml'), rss);\n   329→  console.log('Generated RSS feed');\n   330→}\n   331→\n   332→function generateSitemapEntries(posts) {\n   333→  const entries = posts.filter(p => p.published).map(post => ({\n   334→    url: `/blog/${post.slug}`,\n   335→    lastmod: post.date,\n   336→    changefreq: 'monthly',\n   337→    priority: 0.7\n   338→  }));\n   339→\n   340→  entries.unshift({\n   341→    url: '/blog',\n   342→    lastmod: new Date().toISOString().split('T')[0],\n   343→    changefreq: 'weekly',\n   344→    priority: 0.8\n   345→  });\n   346→\n   347→  fs.writeFileSync(\n   348→    path.join(outputDir, 'sitemap-entries.json'),\n   349→    JSON.stringify(entries, null, 2)\n   350→  );\n   351→  console.log('Generated sitemap entries');\n   352→}\n   353→\n   354→function escapeXml(text) {\n   355→  return text\n   356→    .replace(/&/g, '&amp;')\n   357→    .replace(/</g, '&lt;')\n   358→    .replace(/>/g, '&gt;')\n   359→    .replace(/\"/g, '&quot;')\n   360→    .replace(/'/g, '&apos;');\n   361→}\n   362→\n   363→// Run the build\n   364→buildBlog().catch(console.error);\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"84423f22-f1d0-4544-bb63-fefbb18abb91","uuid":"fa349f9d-d626-446e-ac73-a5a1927e38af","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/airyland/workspace/becool/datetime/scripts/build-blog.js","content":"const fs = require('fs');\nconst path = require('path');\n\nconst contentDir = path.join(process.cwd(), 'content', 'blog');\nconst outputDir = path.join(process.cwd(), 'data', 'blog');\n\n// Ensure output directory exists\nif (!fs.existsSync(outputDir)) {\n  fs.mkdirSync(outputDir, { recursive: true });\n}\n\n// Simple frontmatter parser\nfunction parseFrontmatter(content) {\n  const lines = content.split('\\n');\n  const frontmatter = {};\n  let inFrontmatter = false;\n  let contentStartIndex = 0;\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {\n    const line = lines[i];\n\n    if (i === 0 && line === '---') {\n      inFrontmatter = true;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    if (inFrontmatter && line === '---') {\n      contentStartIndex = i + 1;\n      break;\n    }\n\n    if (inFrontmatter) {\n      const match = line.match(/^(\\w+):\\s*(.*)$/);\n      if (match) {\n        const key = match[1];\n        let value = match[2].trim();\n\n        // Handle arrays (tags)\n        if (value.startsWith('[') && value.endsWith(']')) {\n          value = value.slice(1, -1).split(',').map(v => v.trim().replace(/[\"']/g, ''));\n        } else {\n          // Remove quotes if present\n          value = value.replace(/^[\"']|[\"']$/g, '');\n        }\n\n        frontmatter[key] = value;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  const markdownContent = lines.slice(contentStartIndex).join('\\n');\n  return { frontmatter, content: markdownContent };\n}\n\n// Improved markdown to HTML converter\nfunction markdownToHtml(markdown) {\n  let html = markdown;\n\n  // Preserve code blocks first\n  const codeBlocks = [];\n  html = html.replace(/```([\\s\\S]*?)```/g, (match, code) => {\n    const placeholder = `__CODE_BLOCK_${codeBlocks.length}__`;\n    codeBlocks.push('<pre><code>' + escapeHtml(code.trim()) + '</code></pre>');\n    return placeholder;\n  });\n\n  // Inline code\n  html = html.replace(/`([^`]+)`/g, '<code>$1</code>');\n\n  // Headers (shift levels: # becomes h2, ## becomes h3, etc.)\n  html = html.replace(/^###### (.*?)$/gm, '<h6>$1</h6>');\n  html = html.replace(/^##### (.*?)$/gm, '<h6>$1</h6>');\n  html = html.replace(/^#### (.*?)$/gm, '<h5>$1</h5>');\n  html = html.replace(/^### (.*?)$/gm, '<h4>$1</h4>');\n  html = html.replace(/^## (.*?)$/gm, '<h3>$1</h3>');\n  html = html.replace(/^# (.*?)$/gm, '<h2>$1</h2>');\n\n  // Blockquotes\n  html = html.replace(/^> (.*?)$/gm, '<blockquote>$1</blockquote>');\n\n  // Bold and italic\n  html = html.replace(/\\*\\*\\*(.*?)\\*\\*\\*/g, '<strong><em>$1</em></strong>');\n  html = html.replace(/\\*\\*(.*?)\\*\\*/g, '<strong>$1</strong>');\n  html = html.replace(/\\*(.*?)\\*/g, '<em>$1</em>');\n\n  // Links (add nofollow to external links)\n  html = html.replace(/\\[([^\\]]+)\\]\\(([^)]+)\\)/g, (match, text, url) => {\n    // Check if it's an external link (not starting with / or #)\n    const isExternal = !url.startsWith('/') && !url.startsWith('#') && !url.includes('datetime.app');\n    const rel = isExternal ? ' rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\"' : '';\n    return `<a href=\"${url}\"${rel}>${text}</a>`;\n  });\n\n  // Horizontal rules (but not table separators)\n  html = html.replace(/^---$/gm, '<hr>');\n  html = html.replace(/^\\*\\*\\*$/gm, '<hr>');\n\n  // Process tables and lists\n  const lines = html.split('\\n');\n  let result = [];\n  let inList = false;\n  let listType = '';\n  let inTable = false;\n  let tableRows = [];\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {\n    let line = lines[i];\n\n    // Table detection\n    if (line.match(/^\\|(.+)\\|$/)) {\n      // Check if it's a separator line (contains only |, -, :, and spaces)\n      // A separator line has cells that only contain -, :, and spaces\n      const cells = line.split('|').slice(1, -1);\n      const isSeparator = cells.every(cell => /^[\\s\\-:]+$/.test(cell));\n\n      if (!inTable && !isSeparator) {\n        // Start of table\n        inTable = true;\n        tableRows = [];\n      }\n\n      if (inTable) {\n        if (!isSeparator) {\n          // Parse table row\n          const parsedCells = cells.map(cell => cell.trim());\n          tableRows.push(parsedCells);\n        }\n\n        // Check if next line is not a table row (end of table)\n        if (i + 1 >= lines.length || !lines[i + 1].match(/^\\|(.+)\\|$/)) {\n          // Build HTML table\n          let tableHtml = '<table>\\n';\n\n          if (tableRows.length > 0) {\n            // First row is header\n            tableHtml += '<thead>\\n<tr>\\n';\n            tableRows[0].forEach(cell => {\n              tableHtml += `<th>${cell}</th>\\n`;\n            });\n            tableHtml += '</tr>\\n</thead>\\n';\n\n            // Remaining rows are body\n            if (tableRows.length > 1) {\n              tableHtml += '<tbody>\\n';\n              for (let j = 1; j < tableRows.length; j++) {\n                tableHtml += '<tr>\\n';\n                tableRows[j].forEach(cell => {\n                  tableHtml += `<td>${cell}</td>\\n`;\n                });\n                tableHtml += '</tr>\\n';\n              }\n              tableHtml += '</tbody>\\n';\n            }\n          }\n\n          tableHtml += '</table>';\n          result.push(tableHtml);\n\n          inTable = false;\n          tableRows = [];\n        }\n        continue;\n      }\n    }\n\n    // Unordered list\n    if (line.match(/^[\\*\\-] (.+)$/)) {\n      const content = line.replace(/^[\\*\\-] (.+)$/, '$1');\n      if (!inList || listType !== 'ul') {\n        if (inList) result.push('</' + listType + '>');\n        result.push('<ul>');\n        inList = true;\n        listType = 'ul';\n      }\n      result.push('<li>' + content + '</li>');\n    }\n    // Ordered list\n    else if (line.match(/^\\d+\\. (.+)$/)) {\n      const content = line.replace(/^\\d+\\. (.+)$/, '$1');\n      if (!inList || listType !== 'ol') {\n        if (inList) result.push('</' + listType + '>');\n        result.push('<ol>');\n        inList = true;\n        listType = 'ol';\n      }\n      result.push('<li>' + content + '</li>');\n    }\n    // End of list\n    else {\n      if (inList && line.trim() === '') {\n        result.push('</' + listType + '>');\n        inList = false;\n        listType = '';\n      }\n      result.push(line);\n    }\n  }\n\n  if (inList) {\n    result.push('</' + listType + '>');\n  }\n\n  html = result.join('\\n');\n\n  // Paragraphs\n  const blocks = html.split(/\\n\\n+/);\n  html = blocks.map(block => {\n    block = block.trim();\n    if (block && !block.match(/^<(h[1-6]|ul|ol|li|blockquote|pre|hr|div|p|table|__CODE_BLOCK_)/)) {\n      return '<p>' + block.replace(/\\n/g, ' ') + '</p>';\n    }\n    return block;\n  }).filter(b => b).join('\\n\\n');\n\n  // Restore code blocks\n  codeBlocks.forEach((code, i) => {\n    html = html.replace(`__CODE_BLOCK_${i}__`, code);\n  });\n\n  return html;\n}\n\nfunction escapeHtml(text) {\n  const map = {\n    '&': '&amp;',\n    '<': '&lt;',\n    '>': '&gt;',\n    '\"': '&quot;',\n    \"'\": '&#039;'\n  };\n  return text.replace(/[&<>\"']/g, m => map[m]);\n}\n\nfunction generateSlug(filename) {\n  return filename.replace(/\\.md$/, '').toLowerCase();\n}\n\nfunction generateExcerpt(content, maxLength = 150) {\n  const plainText = content.replace(/<[^>]*>/g, '').replace(/\\n+/g, ' ').trim();\n  if (plainText.length <= maxLength) return plainText;\n  return plainText.substring(0, maxLength).trim() + '...';\n}\n\nfunction estimateReadTime(content) {\n  const wordsPerMinute = 200;\n  const words = content.split(/\\s+/).length;\n  const minutes = Math.ceil(words / wordsPerMinute);\n  return `${minutes} min read`;\n}\n\nasync function buildBlog() {\n  console.log('Building blog data...');\n\n  // Check if content directory exists\n  if (!fs.existsSync(contentDir)) {\n    console.log('No blog content found. Creating directory...');\n    fs.mkdirSync(contentDir, { recursive: true });\n    return;\n  }\n\n  const files = fs.readdirSync(contentDir).filter(file => file.endsWith('.md'));\n\n  const posts = [];\n\n  for (const file of files) {\n    const filePath = path.join(contentDir, file);\n    const fileContent = fs.readFileSync(filePath, 'utf-8');\n    const { frontmatter, content } = parseFrontmatter(fileContent);\n\n    const html = markdownToHtml(content);\n    const slug = frontmatter.slug || generateSlug(file);\n    const excerpt = frontmatter.description || generateExcerpt(content);\n    const readTime = frontmatter.readTime || estimateReadTime(content);\n\n    posts.push({\n      slug,\n      title: frontmatter.title,\n      description: excerpt,\n      date: frontmatter.date,\n      author: frontmatter.author || 'Site Team',\n      tags: Array.isArray(frontmatter.tags) ? frontmatter.tags : (frontmatter.tags ? [frontmatter.tags] : []),\n      keywords: frontmatter.keywords || '',\n      image: frontmatter.image || '/images/blog/default.jpg',\n      readTime,\n      featured: frontmatter.featured === 'true' || frontmatter.featured === true,\n      published: frontmatter.published !== 'false' && frontmatter.published !== false,\n      content: html\n    });\n  }\n\n  // Sort posts by date (newest first)\n  posts.sort((a, b) => new Date(b.date) - new Date(a.date));\n\n  // Write posts data\n  fs.writeFileSync(\n    path.join(outputDir, 'posts.json'),\n    JSON.stringify(posts, null, 2)\n  );\n\n  console.log(`Built ${posts.length} blog posts`);\n\n  // Generate RSS feed\n  generateRSSFeed(posts);\n\n  // Generate sitemap entries\n  generateSitemapEntries(posts);\n}\n\nfunction generateRSSFeed(posts) {\n  const rss = `<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<rss version=\"2.0\" xmlns:atom=\"http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom\">\n  <channel>\n    <title>Datetime.app Blog</title>\n    <description>Expert insights on timezones, time management, and productivity</description>\n    <link>https://datetime.app/blog</link>\n    <atom:link href=\"https://datetime.app/blog/rss.xml\" rel=\"self\" type=\"application/rss+xml\"/>\n    ${posts.filter(p => p.published).slice(0, 20).map(post => `\n    <item>\n      <title>${escapeXml(post.title)}</title>\n      <description>${escapeXml(post.description)}</description>\n      <link>https://datetime.app/blog/${post.slug}</link>\n      <guid>https://datetime.app/blog/${post.slug}</guid>\n      <pubDate>${new Date(post.date).toUTCString()}</pubDate>\n    </item>`).join('')}\n  </channel>\n</rss>`;\n\n  fs.writeFileSync(path.join(outputDir, 'rss.xml'), rss);\n  console.log('Generated RSS feed');\n}\n\nfunction generateSitemapEntries(posts) {\n  const entries = posts.filter(p => p.published).map(post => ({\n    url: `/blog/${post.slug}`,\n    lastmod: post.date,\n    changefreq: 'monthly',\n    priority: 0.7\n  }));\n\n  entries.unshift({\n    url: '/blog',\n    lastmod: new Date().toISOString().split('T')[0],\n    changefreq: 'weekly',\n    priority: 0.8\n  });\n\n  fs.writeFileSync(\n    path.join(outputDir, 'sitemap-entries.json'),\n    JSON.stringify(entries, null, 2)\n  );\n  console.log('Generated sitemap entries');\n}\n\nfunction escapeXml(text) {\n  return text\n    .replace(/&/g, '&amp;')\n    .replace(/</g, '&lt;')\n    .replace(/>/g, '&gt;')\n    .replace(/\"/g, '&quot;')\n    .replace(/'/g, '&apos;');\n}\n\n// Run the build\nbuildBlog().catch(console.error);","numLines":364,"startLine":1,"totalLines":364}}}
