y 15 minutes in 1986 is not entirely clear. However, it's thought to be a result of Nepal's geographic position, placing it between two standard time meridians and a local decision to opt for a middle ground between the two. This placed them away from being directly synced with India's Standard time.\n\n### India (UTC+5:30)\nIndia Standard Time (IST) is UTC+5:30. This is due to India geographically lying between two standard meridians.(no content)India sits right in the middle of a huge landmass. If it picked either UTC+5 or UTC+6, one half of the country would always be out of sync.\n\n### Other Notable Half-Hour and Quarter-Hour Time Zones\n\n- Iran Standard Time (IRST) is UTC+3:30, though daylight saving time can temporarily shift it to UTC+4:30.\n- Afghanistan Time (AFT) is UTC+4:30.\n- The Chatham Islands, a small archipelago belonging to New Zealand, are 45 minutes ahead of New Zealand's mainland and operate at UTC+12:45. Also, there is the Central Western Time Zone of Western Australia, which is UTC+8:45.\n- Some areas in Australia, especially during daylight saving, such as Australian Central Standard Time (ACST) which is at UTC+9:30 when not observing daylight saving, and Australian Central Daylight Time (ACDT) at UTC+10:30 also have half-hour offsets.\n- Eucla in Western Australia is 45 minutes ahead of the rest of the state with its own time zone, called Australian Central Western Standard Time (ACWST). Eucla is also part of the smallest time zone in Australia, which comprises less than 100 people and uses GMT+8:45.\n\n### Why These Unusual Offsets Exist\nThese quirks aren't mistakes. They're deliberate, often steeped in geography, politics, and the push for a bit more daylight or national identity.(no content)Some countries choose non-standard time offsets to better align with daylight hours or for historical and political reasons. Examples include India (UTC+5:30) and Nepal (UTC+5:45).(no content)There are 24 standard time zones in the world, each representing one hour. However, some countries use half-hour or quarter-hour offsets, such as India (UTC+5:30), Nepal (UTC+5:45), and parts of Australia. This means there are actually over 30 unique time offsets in use worldwide."],"durationSeconds":31.704089290999995}}
